- How would you handle deletions or updates to previous elements?
- How would you adapt if the value domain is unbounded or values arrive online without prior compression?

Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a batch-friendly histogram with fast max-tracking, enabling threshold queries without rescanning. It mirrors real-world monitoring workloads where high-volume time-bucketed signals must be maintained with tight per-update costs.

Key Concepts
- Histogram bucketing and width trade-offs
- Segment tree for max-tracking over buckets
- Batch amortization and lazy refresh strategies
- Space efficiency: O(S) vs. O(N)

Code Example
```javascript
class TimeHistogram {
  constructor(T, W) {
    this.S = Math.ceil(T / W); this.W = W; this.counts = new Array(this.S).fill(0);
    // build a simple segment tree for max over counts
    this.seg = new SegmentTree(this.counts);
  }
  apply(t, delta) { const b = Math.floor(t / this.W); this.counts[b] += delta; this.seg.update(b, this.counts[b]); }
  batchUpdate(updates) { for (const {t, d} of updates) this.apply(t, d); }
  hasHeavyBucket(M) { return this.seg.max() >= M; }
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How does changing W affect S and latency? 
- How would you adapt for dynamic T or non-uniform bucket widths?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical amortized analysis for dynamic frequency tracking, demonstrates how to monitor hot keys efficiently, and contrasts incremental updates with full recomputation.

## Key Concepts
- Amortized analysis for batched updates
- Frequency buckets for O(1) max-frequency queries
- Hash maps for per-label counts
- Sparse vs. dense label domains
- Space-time tradeoff analysis

## Code Example
```javascript
class HotKeyTracker {
  constructor(initialCounts) {
    this.cnt = new Map();
    this.freq = new Map();
    this.maxFreq = 0;
    for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(initialCounts)) {
      this.cnt.set(k, v);
      this.freq.set(v, (this.freq.get(v) || 0) + 1);
      this.maxFreq = Math.max(this.maxFreq, v);
    }
  }

  update(label, delta) {
    const old = this.cnt.get(label) || 0;
    const freqCount = this.freq.get(old) || 0;
    if (freqCount === 1) this.freq.delete(old);
    else this.freq.set(old, freqCount - 1);

    const nw = old + delta;
    this.cnt.set(label, nw);
    this.freq.set(nw, (this.freq.get(nw) || 0) + 1);
    this.maxFreq = Math.max(this.maxFreq, nw);
  }

  hasHotKey(threshold) {
    return this.maxFreq >= threshold;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Maintaining the number of distinct substrings under incremental appends tests knowledge of suffix automata, amortized analysis, and practical update strategies. It highlights how to avoid recomputation and reason about state growth.

## Key Concepts
- Suffix Automaton (SAM) for distinct substrings
- Online extensions and delta = len(cur) - len(link(cur))
- Amortized analysis over batches
- Space linear in string length

## Code Example
```javascript
class SAMNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = {};
    this.link = -1;
    this.len = 0;
  }
}
class SuffixAutomaton {
  constructor() {
    this.st = [new SAMNode()];
    this.last = 0;
    this.size = 1;
  }
  extend(c){
    const st = this.st; const cur = this.size++;
    st.push(new SAMNode()); st[cur].len = st[this.last].len + 1;
    let p = this.last;
    while (p >= 0 && !(c in st[p].next)) { st[p].next[c] = cur; p = st[p].link; }
    if (p === -1) { st[cur].link = 0; }
    else {
      const q = st[p].next[c];
      if (st[p].len + 1 === st[q].len) {
        st[cur].link = q;
      } else {
        const clone = this.size++;
        st.push(Object.assign(new SAMNode(), st[q]));
        st[clone].len = st[p].len + 1;
        while (p >= 0 && st[p].next[c] === q) { st[p].next[c] = clone; p = st[p].link; }
        st[q].link = st[cur].link = clone;
      }
    }
    this.last = cur;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle a large alphabet or non-text streams efficiently?
- How does the approach adapt if deletions are allowed?
- What are practical bottlenecks in real-world languages?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates your ability to design streaming top-k algorithms within a sliding window using compact probabilistic data structures and articulate clear trade-offs between accuracy, space, and time.

## Key Concepts

- **Sliding window processing**: Maintaining state only over the most recent W elements
- **Space-Saving sketch**: A fixed-memory algorithm that provides approximate frequency counts
- **Top-k aggregation**: Identifying the k most frequent items in a stream
- **Approximation guarantees**: Bounding error in frequency estimates
- **Time/space complexity**: Analyzing per-operation and batch performance

## Code Example

```javascript
class SlidingTopK {
  constructor(k, W) {
    this.k = k;
    this.W = W;
    this.counters = new Map(); // item -> count
    this.window = []; // queue of last W items
  }

  add(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      this.counters.set(x, this.counters.get(x) + 1);
    } else if (this.counters.size < this.k) {
      this.counters.set(x, 1);
    } else {
      const minEntry = this.findMinCounter();
      this.counters.delete(minEntry.key);
      this.counters.set(x, minEntry.value + 1);
    }
    this.window.push(x);
    if (this.window.length > this.W) {
      this.expire(this.window.shift());
    }
  }

  expire(x) {
    if (this.counters.has(x)) {
      const newCount = this.counters.get(x) - 1;
      if (newCount > 0) {
        this.counters.set(x, newCount);
      } else {
        this.counters.delete(x);
      }
    }
  }

  findMinCounter() {
    let minEntry = { key: null, value: Infinity };
    for (const [key, value] of this.counters) {
      if (value < minEntry.value) {
        minEntry = { key, value };
      }
    }
    return minEntry;
  }

  topK() {
    return [...this.counters.entries()]
      .sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1])
      .slice(0, this.k);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to derive a streaming heavy-hitters approach with bounded memory and clear trade-offs. It focuses on a practical, beginner-friendly variant of the classic Space-Saving problem, tying implementation to batch processing and real-time constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Space-Saving / heavy-hitters
- Fixed-memory streaming
- Per-element vs per-batch costs

## Code Example
